In this lesson we'll look at simulating compound events to estimate probabilities. A compound event is just two or more simple events joined together, like rolling two sixes, getting at least one head in three tosses, or drawing red then blue without replacement. The clever idea is to use random numbers to mimic the experiment instead of working out the theory. You assign numbers to outcomes, say one to six for a die, or zero and one for heads and tails, then generate lots of trials using a random number table, a spreadsheet, or an online tool. After running the simulation, you count how often the compound event showed up and divide by the total number of trials to get your estimated probability. For two dice, you generate two numbers per trial. For three tosses, you generate three. And for drawing without replacement, you just remove each number once it has been drawn so it can't appear again. When you write up your simulation, always describe what you modelled, how many trials you ran, the count of successes, the estimated probability, and compare it to the theoretical value if you know it. Now let's see it in action.